WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.720
Is there a chance Dick Grayson could soon be&nbsp;
making his way to the world of "The Batman"?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.680
What hint did the movie drop&nbsp;
that implied he could? Keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.080
watching to find out about this comics Easter egg.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.480
Director Matt Reeves' "The Batman" delivers&nbsp;
a brand new version of the Caped Crusader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.040
to the big screen. Sure, DC fans are incredibly&nbsp;
familiar with Bruce Wayne's origin after seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.360
it several times in animation and live-action,&nbsp;
but this is a much darker film that takes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.280
precise look at the tortured vigilante a&nbsp;
couple years into his crimefighting career.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.760
The film has already been a huge hit&nbsp;
with critics. Daily Star's Andy Lea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.880
calls it a "gritty, pacy and&nbsp;
achingly stylish adventure,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.560
while The Digital Fix's Tom Percival&nbsp;
says it's "surprisingly hopeful."

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.200
It's fair to say that this new take on Gotham City&nbsp;
does feel grimier than previous iterations of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.760
Bat's home turf. It's stylistically similar&nbsp;
to the world of David Fincher's "Seven," with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.000
hints of "Zodiac" in there for good measure.&nbsp;
This largely stems from Paul Dano's haunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.400
depiction of the Riddler as a terrifying serial&nbsp;
killer who takes aim at the corrupt Gotham elite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.840
Thankfully, Robert Pattinson's Bruce isn't alone&nbsp;
in his mission to serve justice on city streets;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.360
he's also joined by antihero&nbsp;
Catwoman, played by Zoe Kravitz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.640
and police Lieutenant James&nbsp;
Gordon, played by Jeffrey Wright.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.880
The film isn't crammed with Easter eggs like other&nbsp;
comic book movies that dominate the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.080
but there are a few carefully placed references to&nbsp;
classic DC characters. The plot itself takes cues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.560
from classic Batman storylines such as "Year&nbsp;
One," "The Long Halloween," and "Zero Year,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.440
but there's also a surprising nod to one of the&nbsp;
most famous members of the Bat-Family: Nightwing.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.760
By the end of "The Batman," Catwoman, a.k.a.&nbsp;
Selina Kyle, decides that it's time for her to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:36.000
leave Gotham for a while. It's an understandable&nbsp;
move, because part of her arc revolves around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.640
finding who killed her best friend Annika,&nbsp;
and why. To make matters worse, Selina is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.120
forced to confront the fact that she's the&nbsp;
illegitimate daughter of mobster Carmine Falcone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.720
He abandoned Selina's mother after their&nbsp;
brief fling, and she was forced to grow up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.920
in poverty as a result. Falcone, meanwhile,&nbsp;
only grew more powerful. So after she helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.080
Batman stop the Riddler's plan, she tells&nbsp;
the Dark Knight she might head to Bludhaven.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.040
That's a loaded reference for comics readers.&nbsp;
Bludhaven is a city not too far from Gotham.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.240
It's also where Dick Grayson, who&nbsp;
became Nightwing after being Robin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.760
moves in the comics when he starts to&nbsp;
forge his own identity away from Batman.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.680
"Pranks, jokes and tomfoolery have no place here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:19.760
And my identity must reflect that. From&nbsp;
this day forward, call me Nightwing."

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.960
As he soon finds out, Bludhaven&nbsp;
is an incredibly violent place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.200
and there aren't any superheroes operating&nbsp;
there because of how ruthless the villains are.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.880
Nightwing is the one who makes a stand when&nbsp;
Bruce asks him to investigate a string of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.840
deaths in 1996's "Nightwing" issue #1.&nbsp;
It's not enough for Dick to just patrol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.880
the streets at night, though, as he&nbsp;
needs reliable intelligence to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.360
down gangsters like Blockbuster. He even&nbsp;
ends up becoming a police officer by day.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.280
While it's entirely possible that Reeves and&nbsp;
his co-writer Peter Craig will introduce Dick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.440
Grayson further down the line, the Bludhaven&nbsp;
reference probably isn't a hint that Nightwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.080
will show up in "The Batman 2." Even if they&nbsp;
introduce Grayson in the sequel, he'll probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.400
start off as Robin to explore that dynamic with&nbsp;
Batman, rather than stepping into his Nightwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.040
persona straight away. Name-dropping Bludhaven&nbsp;
is more likely just a bit of worldbuilding.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.560
However, that's not to say Nightwing won't&nbsp;
make the leap to the big screen eventually.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.640
Warner Bros. picked "The Tomorrow War" director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.480
Chris McKay to helm a solo movie for&nbsp;
the hero in the DC Extended Universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:19.520
When speaking to Den of Geek back in 2021, McKay&nbsp;
said he still wants to work on the project:

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.200
"I hope that we get a chance to go out and make&nbsp;
that Dick Grayson and Nightwing film. [...] That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.880
particular part of Robin, growing from&nbsp;
being under Batman to becoming Nightwing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.560
and Bludhaven and all that stuff ...&nbsp;
It's a really personal story to me."

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.360
For Nightwing fans desperate&nbsp;
to see the hero in live action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.840
at least HBO Max's "Titans" will scratch&nbsp;
that itch. Brenton Thwaites plays a version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.360
of the hero that's become a real fan&nbsp;
favorite, and it's not hard to see why.

